% lib/feel.le — the built-in functions of FEEL that the other libraries lack.
%
% Need E7 of lpsPlus/docs/migration/roadmap.md (§7.3): the DMN translator
% writes FEEL's built-in functions (the DMN specification, §10.3.4) as
% templates of the shared libraries. lib/text has the length of a text,
% `contains` and `ends with`; lib/rounding has FEEL's decimal(), which
% rounds half to even; lib/temporal has whole years and months between two
% dates, calendar months after a date, and the year and the month of a
% date; lib/maths has the power. This library adds the rest that the
% translated models use: a date written as text, the day of the month,
% substrings and case, the functions of a list, and the remainder.
%
% A library, not syntax: include it as a resource (a translator copies it
% beside the program it writes, with feel.pl), and use its templates like
% any other. Each template that gives one value for its arguments is a
% FUNCTION (§2.3 of the language reference), so it can be an operand of a
% formula.

the knowledge base feel includes these resources:
    feel.pl.

the functions are:
    the date written as *a text* is *a date*.
    the date of year *a number* month *a second number* day *a third number* is *a date*.
    the day of the month of *a date* is *a number*.
    the substring of *a text* from position *a number* with length *a count* is *a part*.
    the substring of *a text* from position *a number* is *a part*.
    the upper case of *a text* is *a second text*.
    the lower case of *a text* is *a second text*.
    the number written as *a text* is *a number*.
    the remainder of *a number* divided by *a divisor* is *a result*.
    the count of the list *a list* is *a number*.
    the sum of the list *a list* is *a number*.
    the minimum of the list *a list* is *a number*.
    the maximum of the list *a list* is *a number*.
    the mean of the list *a list* is *a number*.
    the item at position *a number* of the list *a list* is *a value*.
    today is *a date*.
    the rounding of *a number* away from zero to *a count* decimals is *a result*.
    the rounding of *a number* toward zero to *a count* decimals is *a result*.
    the rounding of *a number* to *a count* decimals half toward zero is *a result*.
    the part of *a text* before *a part* is *a second text*.
    the part of *a text* after *a part* is *a second text*.
    the joining of the list *a list* with *a delimiter* is *a text*.
    the product of the list *a list* is *a number*.
    the median of the list *a list* is *a number*.
    the reverse of the list *a list* is *a second list*.
    the list of the distinct values of *a list* is *a second list*.
    the flattening of the list *a list* is *a second list*.
    the concatenation of the lists *a list* is *a second list*.
    the sublist of *a list* from position *a number* is *a second list*.
    the sublist of *a list* from position *a number* with length *a count* is *a second list*.
    the list *a list* without the item at position *a number* is *a second list*.
    the list *a list* with *a value* inserted at position *a number* is *a second list*.
    the list of the positions of *a value* in *a list* is *a second list*.
    the union of the lists *a list* is *a second list*.
    the conjunction of the list *a list* is *a truth value*.
    the disjunction of the list *a list* is *a truth value*.

the templates are:
    *a list* includes *a value*.
    every item of *a list* is true.
    some item of *a list* is true.
    *a number* is odd.
    *a number* is even.

the knowledge base feel includes:

% Every template is answered by feel.pl.
